## 🔐 Security Audit: `app.js`

### Summary
While the app is small, it contains **5 significant security issues** spanning authentication, data exposure, missing middleware, and denial-of-service risk. Every finding is mapped to the OWASP Top 10 (2021).

---

### 🔴 Finding 1 — Broken Access Control (OWASP A01)
**Location:** `GET /users`

**Problem:** The `/users` endpoint is completely unauthenticated. Any anonymous caller can enumerate all users and their email addresses. There is no authorization check whatsoever.

```js
// ❌ Anyone on the internet can call this
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([{ id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' }, ...]);
});
```

**Fix:** Add an authentication middleware (e.g., JWT or API key verification) and apply it to sensitive routes.

```js
// ✅ Require a valid bearer token
function requireAuth(req, res, next) {
  const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1]; // "Bearer <token>"

  if (!token) {
    return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized: missing token' });
  }

  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET);
    req.user = payload;
    next();
  } catch {
    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden: invalid token' });
  }
}

app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => { ... });
```

---

### 🔴 Finding 2 — Sensitive Data Exposure (OWASP A02 / A04)
**Location:** `GET /users`, `app.listen` console log

**Problem A:** Email addresses are returned in plaintext with zero filtering. In a real app connected to a database, this would expose PII to any caller.

**Problem B:** `console.log('Server running on port 3000')` hard-codes the port and leaks internal infrastructure details to logs that may be aggregated and exposed.

```js
// ❌ Emails exposed with no access control; port hard-coded in logs
app.listen(3000, () => console.log('Server running on port 3000'));
```

**Fix:** Sanitize API responses to return only what callers need, and drive config from environment variables.

```js
// ✅ Omit sensitive fields; read port from env
const PORT = process.env.PORT || 3000;

app.get('/users', requireAuth, (req, res) => {
  // Only return the minimum necessary fields
  const safeUsers = users.map(({ id, name }) => ({ id, name }));
  res.json(safeUsers);
});

app.listen(PORT, () => console.log(`Server started`)); // avoid leaking port in prod
```

---

### 🔴 Finding 3 — Security Misconfiguration (OWASP A05)
**Location:** entire `app.js`

**Problem:** No security headers are set. Express by default advertises itself via the `X-Powered-By: Express` header, enabling fingerprinting. There is also no `Content-Security-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, or `Strict-Transport-Security`.

```js
// ❌ No helmet, X-Powered-By leaks framework info
const app = express();
```

**Fix:** Install and use `helmet`, which sets all standard security headers in one line.

```js
// ✅
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet()); // disables X-Powered-By, sets 11 security headers
```

---

### 🔴 Finding 4 — Missing Input Validation / Injection Risk (OWASP A03)
**Location:** globally — no `express.json()` body parser or any input validation present

**Problem:** There is no `express.json()` or `express.urlencoded()` middleware, meaning if POST/PUT routes are added (the natural next step for this app), raw request bodies will be silently ignored or passed un-parsed. Without a schema validation layer, any future route immediately becomes vulnerable to injection (SQL, NoSQL, command) and prototype pollution.

```js
// ❌ No body parsing, no input validation middleware
const app = express();
app.get('/health', ...);
```

**Fix:** Add body parsing with strict size limits and a validation library.

```js
// ✅ Body parsing with a strict 10 kb size cap to prevent payload-bombing
app.use(express.json({ limit: '10kb' }));
app.use(express.urlencoded({ extended: false, limit: '10kb' }));

// Example validated POST route using express-validator
const { body, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.post('/users',
  requireAuth,
  body('name').isString().trim().isLength({ min: 1, max: 100 }).escape(),
  body('email').isEmail().normalizeEmail(),
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }
    // safe to use req.body here
  }
);
```

---

### 🔴 Finding 5 — Denial of Service / No Rate Limiting (OWASP A04)
**Location:** `GET /health`, `GET /users`

**Problem:** All endpoints are unthrottled. An attacker can send millions of requests per second, exhausting CPU and memory. The `/health` endpoint is especially risky as it is typically public-facing.

```js
// ❌ No rate limiting on any route
app.get('/health', (req, res) => { ... });
```

**Fix:** Add `express-rate-limit`.

```js
// ✅
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100,                  // max 100 requests per window per IP
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' },
});

app.use(limiter); // apply globally, or per-route as needed
```

---

