## Finding #1 — Sensitive Data Exposure: User PII Returned Without Authentication
**OWASP A02 / A01 | Severity: HIGH**

### Problem
`GET /users` returns a full list of user records — including email addresses — to **any caller, unauthenticated, with no rate limiting**. There is no check for who is asking or why.

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE: no auth, no filtering, full PII to the world
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

### Fix
Add an authentication middleware and return only the fields the caller is permitted to see:

```js
// ✅ FIX: authenticate first, then scope the response
const authenticateToken = (req, res, next) => {
  const authHeader = req.headers['authorization'];
  const token = authHeader && authHeader.split(' ')[1]; // Bearer <token>
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Authentication required' });

  jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' });
    req.user = user;
    next();
  });
};

app.get('/users', authenticateToken, (req, res) => {
  // Only return fields appropriate for the caller's role
  const safeUsers = users.map(({ id, name }) => ({ id, name })); // strip email
  res.json(safeUsers);
});
```

---

## Finding #2 — Missing Security Headers
**OWASP A05 | Severity: HIGH**

### Problem
The app sets **no HTTP security headers**. Responses carry no `Content-Security-Policy`, no `X-Frame-Options`, no `X-Content-Type-Options`, etc. Browsers are left unguided and the app is trivially clickjackable and MIME-sniffable.

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE: bare Express with no protective headers
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
```

### Fix
Add `helmet`, which sets 14 security-relevant headers with a single line:

```js
// ✅ FIX: install helmet  →  npm install helmet
const helmet = require('helmet');
app.use(helmet());

// helmet sets, among others:
//   Content-Security-Policy
//   X-Frame-Options: DENY
//   X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
//   Strict-Transport-Security
//   Referrer-Policy
```

---

## Finding #3 — Missing Rate Limiting (Denial-of-Service / Credential Stuffing)
**OWASP A04 / A07 | Severity: HIGH**

### Problem
Every endpoint accepts **unlimited requests per second** from a single IP. This enables trivial DoS and, on any future auth endpoint, brute-force/credential-stuffing attacks.

```js
// ❌ VULNERABLE: no throttling on any route
app.get('/users', (req, res) => { ... });
```

### Fix
```js
// ✅ FIX: install express-rate-limit  →  npm install express-rate-limit
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const globalLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000, // 15 minutes
  max: 100,                  // max 100 requests per window per IP
  standardHeaders: true,
  legacyHeaders: false,
  message: { error: 'Too many requests, please try again later.' },
});

// Stricter limiter for any future auth endpoints
const authLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 10,
  message: { error: 'Too many authentication attempts.' },
});

app.use(globalLimiter);               // apply globally
app.use('/auth', authLimiter);        // extra-strict on auth routes
```

